WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.670
Film Courage: Lee I think you said that feature
writers should have a new, ready-to-market

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:07.250
feature script every six months or so.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:11.789
And that TV writers should have a new original
pilot one every three or four months.

00:00:11.789 --> 00:00:13.059
Lee Jessup: In a perfect world.

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:14.059
Yes…

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:15.059
Film Courage: Is that a lot of pressure though?

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:16.059
Lee Jessup: That’s a lot of pressure.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:19.119
I find that most features go six to twelve
months.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:23.090
You know the truth of the matter is if you
take 14 months and you come up with a great

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:26.330
sample, I’m not going to hold it against
you.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:30.520
The important thing for me is that you keep
writing and keep finishing work.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:35.989
For pilots in a perfect world, I would like
to see two generated in a year.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:37.780
Sometimes it happens that it’s only one.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.590
Sometimes it happens that it’s three.

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:40.739
It really depends on the writer.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:45.150
So there isn’t really one set course that
you’re supposed to follow.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:49.329
But it’s really about doing the work and
getting the work out there.

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:52.530
Sometimes you’ll work on a script and you’ll
say “I’ve worked on this for eight months.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.629
I’ve tackled this every which way, it’s
not happening.”

00:00:55.629 --> 00:01:00.899
It’s also knowing when to let something
go, not for all eternity but for potentially

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:05.920
you can come back to it later when you’ve
had a stroke of genius that informs you how

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.770
to take the work, how to make it better.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:15.909
But the point with having those broad goals
is just do the work and get it out there rather

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:20.501
than spend years and years and years tinkering
with one screenplay that then you run the

00:01:20.501 --> 00:01:24.330
risk that after three years (and I just went
through this with a writer), tinkering with

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.950
the script for three years and then going
“Oh, it’s not good enough.”

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:27.950
It will never be good enough.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.380
I’m not going to get it out there.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.829
And then starting another script and spending
another two years on something and then going

00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:34.240
“Oh but it’s still not good enough.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.080
I’m not going to get this out there.”

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:37.650
That is the challenge that we run into.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.939
So because of that you really want to expose
the work early and often.

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:48.369
So that means from concept, you want to run
the concept by people in the know, whether

00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:52.030
it be members of your writers group or in
a class or whatever it is.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.500
Have people look at your outline.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:58.799
Really don’t be afraid to expose the work
to get feedback to have questions asked.

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:05.310
I was just sitting with a writer of mine who
had written a feature script with a guy that

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.490
is his manager and they had been on the script
for a year and a half.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:13.910
And then he gave the script to a few writer
friends.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.430
And he got some massive notes back.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.220
And what he’d said to me was “Oh, if only
we’d given it an outline.”

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.140
It was what we all were pushing him to do
but he felt loyal to the manager.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.140
He didn't want to expose it.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.180
And I understand that choice that he made.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.090
He was sort of in a tough spot to serve [or]
to listen and to serve the one master.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:32.410
Do you open yourself up?

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:37.200
I generally prefer to expose work early and
often because I just think that it saves you

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.400
a lot of time in the end.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:38.452
Question for the Viewers: How many scripts
did you write last year?

